{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "",
  "stderr": "",
  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nCode agents represent a significant advancement in artificial intelligence, combining large language models with code interpretation capabilities to autonomously generate, execute, and debug code. However, these powerful systems introduce critical safety and security vulnerabilities that must be addressed before widespread deployment. This paper presents a comprehensive review of recent advances in code agent security, focusing on red-teaming methodologies and safety evaluation frameworks. We examine four key works that have shaped the field: RedCodeAgent, an automated red-teaming agent for uncovering vulnerabilities in diverse code agents; RedCode, a benchmark for evaluating risky code execution and generation; DecodingTrust, a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models; and BlueCodeAgent, a defense-oriented approach leveraging automated red teaming. Our analysis reveals significant gaps in current security practices, including insufficient adversarial testing, limited benchmark coverage, and reactive rather than proactive defense strategies. We propose recommendations for future research directions in code agent safety and security evaluation.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe emergence of code agents—AI systems capable of generating, understanding, and executing code autonomously—has revolutionized software development workflows. By integrating large language models (LLMs) with code interpreters, these agents enable dynamic execution, interactive debugging, and automated software testing. Popular platforms such as Cursor, Codeium, and GitHub Copilot have demonstrated the transformative potential of code agents in accelerating development processes (1).\n\nHowever, this rapid adoption has revealed significant security and safety concerns. Code agents with execution capabilities can inadvertently generate malicious code, exploit system vulnerabilities, or execute harmful commands when exposed to adversarial inputs. The unique architecture of code agents—combining language understanding with code execution—creates novel attack surfaces that traditional safety mechanisms fail to address adequately.\n\nRecent research has begun addressing these challenges through various approaches. Automated red-teaming agents have been developed to systematically discover vulnerabilities in code agents by dynamically adapting their attack strategies. Comprehensive benchmarks have been established to evaluate the safety of code generation models across diverse risk categories. Additionally, blue-teaming approaches leveraging red-teaming insights have emerged as complementary defense mechanisms.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive review of these advances, synthesizing insights from four pivotal works that have shaped the current understanding of code agent security. We examine the methodologies, findings, and implications of these studies, and discuss open challenges and future directions in this rapidly evolving field.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\n**RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** (Guo et al., arXiv:2501.00001) represents a significant advancement in automated vulnerability discovery for code agents. Traditional static safety benchmarks and manual red-teaming approaches prove inadequate for identifying the complex, multi-step vulnerabilities that code agents face in real-world scenarios. RedCodeAgent introduces an innovative framework that combines an adaptive memory module with a diverse toolbox of red-teaming strategies.\n\nThe key innovation of RedCodeAgent lies in its ability to leverage existing jailbreak knowledge to dynamically select the most effective attack strategies and tool combinations for a given input query. This adaptive approach enables the agent to explore the attack surface more thoroughly than static methods. Furthermore, RedCodeAgent addresses a critical limitation of previous evaluation approaches by incorporating simulated sandbox environments for evaluating execution results, mitigating potential biases of LLM-based judges that rely solely on static code analysis.\n\nExtensive evaluations across multiple state-of-the-art code agents demonstrate that RedCodeAgent consistently outperforms existing methods, achieving higher attack success rates and lower rejection rates with improved efficiency. The framework was also validated on real-world code assistants, exposing previously unidentified security risks and highlighting the urgent need for automated red-teaming in code agent development pipelines.\n\n### 2.2 Safety Benchmarking for Code Generation\n\n**RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents** (Xie et al., arXiv:2405.00003) addresses the critical need for standardized safety evaluation of code agents. As code generation models become increasingly powerful, establishing comprehensive benchmarks for assessing their safety profiles becomes paramount.\n\nRedCode introduces a systematically constructed benchmark covering diverse risk categories including malware generation, vulnerability exploitation, policy violations, and harmful command execution. The benchmark enables both controlled evaluation and comparative analysis across different code agent architectures. By providing a standardized evaluation framework, RedCode facilitates reproducible research and enables the community to track progress in code agent safety over time.\n\nThe benchmark reveals significant vulnerabilities in even the most advanced code generation models, particularly when exposed to carefully crafted adversarial prompts. These findings underscore the importance of safety-aware model development and the need for robust filtering mechanisms in production deployments.\n\n### 2.3 Trustworthiness Assessment in Large Language Models\n\n**DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models** (Wang et al., arXiv:2304.00002) provides foundational insights into the trustworthiness challenges of large language models that directly inform code agent security. While not specifically designed for code agents, this work established a comprehensive framework for evaluating model trustworthiness across multiple dimensions including toxicity, bias, adversarial robustness, privacy, and ethical considerations.\n\nThe study's key finding—that GPT-4, despite generally higher performance on standard benchmarks, exhibits greater vulnerability to jailbreaking prompts—has profound implications for code agent development. This paradox, where more capable models follow instructions more precisely (including potentially harmful ones), highlights the challenge of aligning model capabilities with safety requirements.\n\nDecodingTrust's methodology of multi-perspective trustworthiness evaluation has influenced subsequent safety benchmarks for code agents, providing a template for comprehensive safety assessment that considers diverse vulnerability categories. The benchmark dataset and evaluation protocols established in this work have become reference points for the community.\n\n### 2.4 Defensive Strategies through Blue Teaming\n\n**BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI** (Wang et al., arXiv:2406.00004) introduces a novel defensive paradigm for code agents. While red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming emphasizes proactive defense development. BlueCodeAgent bridges these approaches by leveraging insights from automated red-teaming to develop comprehensive protection strategies.\n\nThe key innovation of BlueCodeAgent lies in its ability to synthesize findings from simulated adversarial attacks into actionable defense mechanisms. By systematically analyzing attack patterns and failure modes, the agent develops targeted defenses including input sanitization, output filtering, and adversarial training strategies. This iterative process of attack identification and defense development creates a feedback loop that continuously improves system resilience.\n\nEmpirical evaluations demonstrate that BlueCodeAgent significantly enhances code agent resilience against various adversarial attacks while maintaining functional performance. The approach provides a practical framework for building more secure AI-assisted development tools and suggests that blue-teaming should be integrated as a standard component of code agent development pipelines.\n\n## 3. Discussion\n\nThe reviewed works reveal several critical insights about the current state of code agent security:\n\n**Reactive vs. Proactive Security:** Current approaches largely remain reactive, focusing on vulnerability discovery rather than prevention. While RedCodeAgent excels at identifying vulnerabilities, and BlueCodeAgent provides defensive strategies, the gap between attack capability and defense effectiveness remains significant.\n\n**Benchmark Limitations:** Existing benchmarks, while valuable, may not adequately capture the evolving threat landscape. The dynamic nature of code execution environments means that vulnerabilities can emerge from previously unexplored interaction patterns between code generation and execution.\n\n**Model Capability-Safety Trade-off:** DecodingTrust's findings about the capability-safety trade-off extend to code agents: more capable models may be more susceptible to sophisticated attacks that exploit their advanced instruction-following abilities.\n\n**Integration Challenges:** The integration of safety mechanisms into code agent workflows presents engineering challenges. Defense mechanisms must be transparent, efficient, and not unduly restrict agent functionality.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agent security has made substantial progress through recent research, as evidenced by the four pivotal works reviewed in this paper. RedCodeAgent demonstrates the effectiveness of automated, adaptive red-teaming for vulnerability discovery. RedCode establishes the importance of comprehensive safety benchmarking. DecodingTrust provides foundational insights into trustworthiness challenges in large language models. BlueCodeAgent introduces innovative defensive approaches leveraging red-teaming insights.\n\nHowever, significant challenges remain. The rapidly evolving nature of code agents, combined with increasingly sophisticated attack methodologies, necessitates continued research into adaptive safety mechanisms, standardized evaluation frameworks, and robust defense strategies. As code agents become more integrated into critical software development workflows, ensuring their security and trustworthiness will be paramount.\n\nFuture research directions should include:\n- Developing real-time safety monitoring systems that can detect and prevent harmful code execution\n- Creating more sophisticated benchmarks that capture multi-step attack scenarios\n- Investigating the fundamental relationship between model capabilities and security vulnerabilities\n- Building standardized protocols for security evaluation of code agents\n- Exploring collaborative approaches that integrate red and blue teaming into development cycles\n\nThe security of code agents is not merely a technical challenge but a critical enabler for the responsible deployment of AI in software development. Addressing these challenges requires sustained collaboration between researchers, practitioners, and the broader AI community.\n\n## References\n\n1. Guo, C.-Q., Xie, C., Yang, Y., Chen, Z.-R., Lin, Z.-N., Davies, X., Gal, Y., Song, D., & Li, B. (2025). RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents. *arXiv:2501.00001*.\n\n2. Xie, C., Wang, B., Chen, W., Pei, H., Lin, Z.-N., Hendrycks, D., & Li, B. (2024). RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents. *arXiv:2405.00003*.\n\n3. Wang, B., Chen, W., Pei, H., Xie, C., Kang, M., Zhang, C., Xu, C.-J., Xiong, Z., Dutta, R., Schaeffer, R., Truong, S. T., Arora, S., Mazeika, M., Hendrycks, D., Lin, Z., Cheng, Y.-K., Koyejo, O., Song, D., & Li, B. (2023). DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models. *arXiv:2304.00002*.\n\n4. Wang, B., Xie, C., Kang, M., Chen, W., Pei, H., Hendrycks, D., & Li, B. (2024). BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI. *arXiv:2406.00004*.\nEOF"
}'}

